= Error compounding in sequential systems — Simple =

'''Error compounding''' happens when one mistake changes what happens next, making later mistakes more likely or more damaging.

For example:

<pre>
wrong first step
→ different situation
→ next decision uses that situation
→ another mistake becomes possible
</pre>

This can happen in forecasts, robots, language generation, planning, and AI-agent workflows.

But errors do not always grow. A system can correct them with new measurements, validation, feedback, or a return to trusted state.

The important question is whether earlier outputs become later inputs.

